+# Why production hits `mainnet.base.org` and how to settle it
+
+## The exact chain
+
+**1. `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is inlined at build time, not read at runtime.**
+Next.js does not read `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` in the browser. During
+`next build`, webpack **statically string-replaces** every
+`process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_*` reference with the literal value present in the
+build environment's shell at that moment, then bakes it into the JS bundle.
+There is no server-side `process.env` lookup afterward — especially not for a
+static export on generic hosting, where no Node process runs at all. The value
+is frozen into the shipped `.js` chunks.
+
+**2. Dev vs. prod diverge because the value comes from *the build host's* env.**
+- Dev machines: the developer has `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` in their local
+  `.env.local` / shell, so `yarn start` (or `next dev`) inlines the paid URL.
+  Everything routes through the paid RPC → no 429s.
+- Production: the CI/build step that produced the static bundle **did not have
+  `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` set**. So the compiler inlined it as `undefined`.
+  The `.env.local` on the deploy host is irrelevant — it's never consulted by a
+  static export. The paid URL simply never entered the bundle.
+
+**3. Undefined override collapses `rpcOverrides`.**
+```ts
+rpcOverrides: { [chains.base.id]: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL }
+// becomes, in the prod bundle:
+rpcOverrides: { [chains.base.id]: undefined }
+```
+
+**4. The transport falls back to the chain's default public RPC.**
+In `wagmiConfig.tsx`'s `client({ chain })`, the override is read as
+`rpcOverrideUrl = scaffoldConfig.rpcOverrides?.[chain.id]`. It's `undefined`, so
+the `if (rpcOverrideUrl)` branch is skipped and `rpcFallbacks` stays/ends up as
+a bare `[http()]` (or public-first). **`http()` called with no URL uses
+`chain.rpcUrls.default.http[0]`**, which for Base is exactly
+`https://mainnet.base.org`. Every production request pours onto the public
+endpoint → 429s.
+
+## The one operational check that settles whether the fix is real
+
+**Grep the deployed production bundle (or watch the prod DevTools Network tab)
+for the RPC hostname — do not trust source, `.env`, or a redeploy.**
+
+Because the URL is inlined, the truth is in the shipped artifact:
+
+```bash
+# against the built/deployed static assets:
+grep -r "your-paid-rpc-host" .next/  # or curl the live .js chunks
+grep -r "mainnet.base.org" .next/
+```
+
+If the paid host appears as a string literal → the env var was present **at
+build time** and the fix is real. If you still see `mainnet.base.org` /
+`undefined` → the rebuild didn't have the var. This distinguishes the only two
+failure modes that a config diff can't: (a) redeploying the *same* bundle
+without rebuilding, and (b) rebuilding in a CI environment that still lacks the
+var. Equivalently: load production, open the Network tab, and confirm the
+JSON-RPC POSTs go to the paid host, not `mainnet.base.org`.
+
+## The second, transport-level trap in `wagmiConfig.tsx`
+
+Even once the env var is fixed and the override is present, the transport is:
+
+```ts
+rpcFallbacks = [http(rpcOverrideUrl), http()];   // note the bare http()
+return createClient({ chain, transport: fallback(rpcFallbacks) });
+```
+
+The bare **`http()` (= public `mainnet.base.org`) is permanently kept in the
+`fallback()` chain** as the second transport. viem's `fallback` transport
+**fails over to the next transport on any error from the primary — including a
+429**. So under load, when the paid RPC rate-limits or times out, requests
+silently roll onto the public endpoint anyway (and with `rank` enabled, viem
+also periodically probes the ranked transports, touching the public RPC even
+when it isn't primary). The public RPC is never actually removed from rotation;
+fixing the env var only changes *which* transport is tried first.
+
+**Related consequence (same root):** Scaffold-ETH always appends `mainnet`
+(chain id 1) to `enabledChains` for ENS / ETH-price resolution, but
+`rpcOverrides` only keys `base.id`. So all mainnet requests hit `http()` with no
+override — Ethereum's default public RPC — regardless of the Base fix. If you
+want zero public-RPC traffic, add an override for **every** enabled chain and
+drop/replace the bare `http()` failover.

